  {
    "episode_id": 80104,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80104.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I mean, it's actually the very last bill that I introduced just days before I left Congress. I introduced with Ken Buck. And I'm sorry, the last bill of the last Congress that I completed. And we did it just four or five days before Congress adjourned because we wanted to put a marker down. And it was basically a good bill to, there was a decision made in the second circuit where someone was killed by Hamas. terrorists and they sued and they sued Facebook because they met in a terrorist chatroom and the court dismissed and said Section 230 gives liability to Facebook. There was a judge who wrote a dissenting opinion in that case and said, wait a minute. Section 230 applies to the initial posting that gets put on Facebook. That's covered. But then Facebook makes a set of business decisions to apply a set of algorithms that will amplify that content and maximize its commercial value to Facebook. And for those decisions, Section 230 doesn't apply. And so we took that decision and basically wrote a piece of legislation that said, look, what gets posted, we recognize there's a first amendment issue, you posted. But then a business says, I'm going to amplify this in a way to make a bunch of money. And if you do that, you're responsible for the harm that comes from it. So I think that's actually a perfect solution of a way you can shift responsibility for the business decisions that these platforms make to amplify this toxic content. And with AI, there's no reason it should apply as well.",
